\f1\fs22 Johnny ignored the waiting line of ducktail and pompadour haircuts, the leather jackets and the jumpsuits. He dismissed the protests of the clip-boarded hostess and the muscle-corded bouncer, and strode purposefully and persuasively inside. He fought back alternating expressions of horror and disgust as he feared he\'92d never cleanse himself of revisiting such a place, the 
\f2\i Blue Shade
\f1\i0 .\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I don\'92t know why I\'92m here,\'92 he muttered to himself, but he knew with sickening and hopeful certainty, as he fought through the crowd and approached the stool where Sarah had sat and the one alongside where he\'92d perched as light as he could, fearful of the bacteria infesting it. Both stools were unoccupied\'97strange considering the standing-room only crush of the place. Johnny sighed; the forlorn and delusional part of him hoped he\'92d find Sarah. Instead, that part shrivelled in shame for its eternal adulation.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>As Johnny sat, a bartender replenished the complimentary bowls of peanuts and bacon.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What\'92ll it be?\'92 the bartender drawled.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91House special \'85 
\f2\i Hunk of Burning Coal
\f1\i0 .\'92 Though Johnny had no idea his sweat smelled of rancid fat from his privileged consumption of excess animal proteins, he\'92d certainly notice the aroma of peanut butter in his bed chamber in the morning.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91We don\'92t call it that any more,\'92 the bar tender said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Whatever. Just rack it up and keep them coming.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91It\'92s called 
\f2\i Funk of Burning Blood
\f1\i0 ,\'92 the bartender said with born-again enthusiasm.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I don\'92t give a tax.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The bartender pulled a what-smells-bad face. \'91You don\'92t understand. They\'92re the Word of the Great Teacher: he gave us new lyrics.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Who cares? Pop songs are meaningless drivel about love and puppies and how lame your last love interest is.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Thanks, Sailor Swift. What a killjoy.\'92 The bartender shook the cocktail. \'91Thing is, now we\'92re not punching each other up over lyrics, we\'92re recording all the official songs. I\'92m surprised you haven\'92t heard this one yet. Even the Airborne are streaming it.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I\'92d rather listen to a stream of bat\'92s piss.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The bartender stop assembling the cocktail. \'91You want your drink or not?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Johnny grimaced. \'91Yeah, gimme.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The bartender noticed Johnny\'92s shoulders rounding. \'91Hard day at the office?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Yeah, I \'97 an employee blew the place up.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91We get a lot of that around here.\'92 The bartender poured the cocktail into an obscenely large glass, and topped whole thing with honey and peanut butter. \'91For an authentic experience, crumble in some bacon.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Johnny gulped hard. \'91You should demand to see a customer\'92s medical insurance before serving this.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Ha, that\'92s funny,\'92 the bartender said. \'91Oh, look! His lordship\'92s got medical insurance,\'92 he mocked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A round of applause erupted as the first performer took the stage. Johnny looked across the space. The crowd seemed so much happier than last time. The performer\'97Johnny couldn\'92t place what Elvi series it came from\'97grabbed the mike. The crowd whooped and cheered. When they settled a little the performer said, \'91I\'92d just like to acknowledge the Great Teacher for giving us these words.\'92 The crowd erupted. \'91This one goes out to all the venture capitalists out there.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Cheers and cries of \'91Woo! Venture capitalists.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Jonny raised his glass and whispered, \'91To Sarah. Let\'92s make this fast then,\'92 and gulped.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91It\'92s called 
\f2\i The Blunder of You
\f1\i0 .\'92 The band struck up a tune and washed away the noise of the hushing crowd, a tune which, in a suspiciously high number of universes more stable than this, would be instantly recognisable.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Johnny\'92s tablet vibrated. He took it out and read its face, a message from Eloise Shakti: 
\f2\i My office, tomorrow 8am SHARP
